### Part 2: `StringBuffer` (Mutable & Thread-Safe) 
*(This is what you specifically asked for!)*

- **Definition:** A mutable sequence of characters.
- **Thread-Safe:** All critical methods are **synchronized**. This means it is safe to use when multiple threads are accessing it simultaneously. 
- **Performance:** Slower than `StringBuilder` due to the synchronization overhead, but safer for multi-threading.

#### `StringBuffer` Common Methods

| Method | Description | Example |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`append(str)`** | Adds text to the end of the buffer. | `sb.append(" World");` |
| **`insert(offset, str)`** | Inserts text at a specific position. | `sb.insert(6, "Java ");` |
| **`reverse()`** | Reverses the character sequence. | `sb.reverse();` // "Java" → "avaJ" |
| **`delete(start, end)`** | Removes characters from start to end-1. | `sb.delete(5, 10);` |
| **`toString()`** | Converts the mutable buffer into an immutable `String`. | `String result = sb.toString();` |

**Code Example (Putting it together):**
```java
StringBuffer sb = new StringBuffer("Hello");
sb.append(" World");        // Hello World
sb.insert(6, "Java ");      // Hello Java World
sb.delete(5, 10);           // Hello World (removes " Java")
System.out.println(sb.toString()); 
```

