### Part 3: `StringBuilder` (Mutable & NOT Thread-Safe)
- **Definition:** Identical to `StringBuffer` in syntax and features.
- **Not Thread-Safe:** Methods are **not synchronized**. 
- **Performance:** Faster than `StringBuffer` because it has no locking overhead. 
- **Use Case:** Use this when you are working in a **single-threaded** environment (which is most typical applications).

#### `StringBuilder` Methods
*(They are exactly the same as StringBuffer, so refer to the table above!)*
- `append()`, `insert()`, `reverse()`, `delete()`, `toString()`.

---

### 🔥 The "Big Three" Comparison Table (Crucial for Exams/Interviews)

| Feature | `String` | `StringBuffer` | `StringBuilder` |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Mutability** | **Immutable** (Cannot change) | **Mutable** (Can change) | **Mutable** (Can change) |
| **Thread-Safe** | Yes (due to immutability) | **Yes** (Synchronized) | **No** (Not synchronized) |
| **Performance** | Slowest (creates new objects) | Medium (overhead of sync) | **Fastest** (no sync) |
| **When to use?** | When value doesn't change (e.g., keys, constants). | When shared across multiple threads. | When used inside a single method/thread. |
| **Memory** | Stored in **String Pool** | Stored in **Heap** | Stored in **Heap** |

